> Displacement, at least, gives pretty much the same benefit

Similar, but much weaker.

My experience with displacement is that if the monster is more than a 
couple squares from you, it won't "swing wildly"; instead it will proceed 
towards your apparent location (or use a ranged attack).  Even when it 
does "swing wildly", if it swings at an empty space, you don't get the 
benefit of it damaging another monster.

Furthermore, "1/4 chance of finding you anyway, and remembering your 
location until you move away." (intr-343).  That could be inconvenient 
when you're surrounded...  

Meanwhile conflict works against all monsters in range (8 squares IIRC).  
Every turn each gets a MR roll to resist, and if it fails it will attack 
at randomly (possibly you if you're adjacent).  If it manages to resist, 
it will act normally -- this turn.  If it has a lot of MR, it will resist 
a lot, but maybe still die to its stronger and dumber buddy.  Picture it:

Minotaur: Kill! Kill!
Arch-Lich: No, fool! Kill *him*!
Minotaur: Roar! Smash!
Arch-Lich: /crumbles into dust

Here's a good example of the difference between conflict and 
teleportation:

